WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:05.660
“In the beginning there was nothing, which
exploded”

00:00:05.660 --> 00:00:11.459
That’s how the great Terry Pratchett described
the beginning of the universe; the legendary

00:00:11.459 --> 00:00:12.750
big bang.

00:00:12.750 --> 00:00:16.810
The majority of modern physics comes from
the understanding that everything started

00:00:16.810 --> 00:00:24.070
out as one tiny tiny point that blew up, expanded
and created all of the wonderful things around

00:00:24.070 --> 00:00:27.390
you like galaxies, stars, planets and bacon
sandwiches.

00:00:27.390 --> 00:00:33.640
As a planet, we’re pretty set on this concept
but that’s not to say it’s the only possibility

00:00:33.640 --> 00:00:35.420
that’s been suggested.

00:00:35.420 --> 00:00:40.860
Obviously you have the many religious creation
stories but even within the scientific community,

00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:44.030
a few other options have been suggested.

00:00:44.030 --> 00:00:48.870
So if you’ve ever wondered whether our universe
is actually all a cat’s dream, a marble

00:00:48.870 --> 00:00:54.360
in the hand of a giant alien or a simulation
that has some worrying coding errors, well

00:00:54.360 --> 00:01:01.719
then here are some alternative and entirely
possible theories to the big bang.

00:01:01.719 --> 00:01:10.110
Our first idea was born out of string theory
and popularised by Steinhardt and Turok, who

00:01:10.110 --> 00:01:12.640
sound like a German techno duo.

00:01:12.640 --> 00:01:18.159
String theory is basically an attempt to connect
the ideas of general relativity with the bizarre

00:01:18.159 --> 00:01:21.270
and confusing world of quantum mechanics.

00:01:21.270 --> 00:01:26.560
The idea is that there are many more dimensions
than the ones we can observe and that something

00:01:26.560 --> 00:01:33.549
that might look like a point from our perspective,
is actually joint a part of a long string,

00:01:33.549 --> 00:01:38.770
like you are looking at a bit of spaghetti
dead on and mistaking it for a tiny speck.

00:01:38.770 --> 00:01:43.150
Steinhardt and Turok proposed an idea called
the Incredible Bulk.

00:01:43.150 --> 00:01:49.240
It’s not been entirely rejected by the physics
community, but possible because they are afraid

00:01:49.240 --> 00:01:53.700
of making the incredible bulk angry, you wouldn’t
like it when it’s angry.

00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:58.390
It suggests that rather than there being one
big bang, our observable universe is like

00:01:58.390 --> 00:02:02.810
a sheet of paper drifting in a multi-dimensional
soup.

00:02:02.810 --> 00:02:06.750
Theorists call these sheets branes, short
for membrane.

00:02:06.750 --> 00:02:11.830
Occasionally our brane collides with another
brane and there is a big release of energy.

00:02:11.830 --> 00:02:15.761
There is an endless cycle of collisions and
expanding branes.

00:02:15.761 --> 00:02:20.900
Mmm brane soup.

00:02:20.900 --> 00:02:24.450
Sean Carrol has been obsessed with time since
he was young, probably as a way to explain

00:02:24.450 --> 00:02:25.450
why he was always late for dinner.

00:02:25.450 --> 00:02:30.030
His trouble with the big bang was not that
it came at the beginning of time but that

00:02:30.030 --> 00:02:36.550
time itself has a beginning and end; why does
time only move in one direction?

00:02:36.550 --> 00:02:44.840
Well what if there were multiple universes
and in some of them, time runs backwards?

00:02:44.840 --> 00:02:52.820
In the Multiverse theory, a universe can spring
up from the random fluctuations in dark energy,

00:02:52.820 --> 00:02:56.910
which appears throughout what we think of
as empty space.

00:02:56.910 --> 00:03:00.680
Dark energy is like glitter, it gets bloody
everywhere.

00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:07.660
Sometimes the universes wouldn’t really
get going and they’d collapse into a black

00:03:07.660 --> 00:03:08.660
hole.

00:03:08.660 --> 00:03:13.590
But occasionally one takes off and expands
rapidly.

00:03:13.590 --> 00:03:18.460
It keeps expanding forever and eventually
becomes so thinned out that it is now the

00:03:18.460 --> 00:03:23.730
empty space where another universe can form.

00:03:23.730 --> 00:03:31.370
One of the confusing things about the universe
is that it looks basically the same in all

00:03:31.370 --> 00:03:34.370
directions, no matter where you’re looking
from.

00:03:34.370 --> 00:03:38.720
Since we know the universe is expanding, we
tend to think of it as a balloon but for most

00:03:38.720 --> 00:03:44.620
of us, this give the idea of a centre and
an edge, which is not really the case.

00:03:44.620 --> 00:03:49.760
Fred Hoyle tried to explain this similarity
of all parts of the universe with an idea

00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:51.950
called Steady State.

00:03:51.950 --> 00:03:57.000
If you want to keep the density of stuff the
same in an expanding space, surely you need

00:03:57.000 --> 00:03:59.540
to keep adding things.

00:03:59.540 --> 00:04:04.880
It’s like a house party, if people keep
arriving, you’re going to need to keep adding

00:04:04.880 --> 00:04:08.680
booze, otherwise everyone will either start
getting bored or possibly drinking all your

00:04:08.680 --> 00:04:13.110
perfume and cleaning products and that won’t
end well.

00:04:13.110 --> 00:04:17.850
In order to keep the density up, matter would
need to be spontaneously created.

00:04:17.850 --> 00:04:22.770
And before you start picturing random bubbles
of hydrogen appearing all around you, the

00:04:22.770 --> 00:04:31.750
amount suggested was about one atom a year
within each cubic kilometre, so no need to

00:04:31.750 --> 00:04:40.530
squeeze up just yet.

00:04:40.530 --> 00:04:46.140
Speaking of running out of space, in Christoff
Wetterich’s theory, we are all heading towards

00:04:46.140 --> 00:04:52.390
a big game of galactic sardines, since rather
than expanding, the walls are actually closing

00:04:52.390 --> 00:04:53.390
in.

00:04:53.390 --> 00:04:56.260
He called it the cold bang.

00:04:56.260 --> 00:05:00.820
One of the big observable factors for why
we believe the universe is expanding is the

00:05:00.820 --> 00:05:03.460
Doppler Effect, or red shift.

00:05:03.460 --> 00:05:09.350
We noticed that very distant galaxies seemed
to be redder than closer ones, and this was

00:05:09.350 --> 00:05:11.300
true whichever way we looked.

00:05:11.300 --> 00:05:16.070
So unless we were surrounded by a gigantic
red light district, there must be some reason

00:05:16.070 --> 00:05:17.070
for this.

00:05:17.070 --> 00:05:18.070
And there is.

00:05:18.070 --> 00:05:23.840
If an object is giving out a wave, like light
or sound, then its movement affects how you,

00:05:23.840 --> 00:05:26.030
the observer receives that wave.

00:05:26.030 --> 00:05:30.720
If it’s moving towards you, then the waves
are pushed together at a higher frequency.

00:05:30.720 --> 00:05:36.050
If the object is moving away, then they are
stretched out.

00:05:36.050 --> 00:05:40.170
You can hear this effect with a police siren,
the pattern repeats quicker as the siren comes

00:05:40.170 --> 00:05:46.330
near, and then seems to slow down as it moves
off into the distance, unless you’re being

00:05:46.330 --> 00:05:49.160
arrested, in which case you’ll only get
the first part.

00:05:49.160 --> 00:05:55.190
So, if the universe is expanding, then light
from distant objects is being stretched since

00:05:55.190 --> 00:06:00.130
they are moving away, so it has a longer wavelength,
making it redder.

00:06:00.130 --> 00:06:04.810
Wetterich countered this by saying that as
the universe closes in, the light would be

00:06:04.810 --> 00:06:10.320
affected by the greater mass of the universe.

00:06:10.320 --> 00:06:18.070
The benefit of his theory is that ait voids
the need for everything starting from a singularity.

00:06:18.070 --> 00:06:21.740
The problem with it is that there is going
to be much less space on the beach in a few

00:06:21.740 --> 00:06:24.060
trillion years.

00:06:24.060 --> 00:06:29.670
Another factor of existence that is difficult
to explain is why everywhere is a relatively

00:06:29.670 --> 00:06:30.750
even temperature.

00:06:30.750 --> 00:06:36.030
Sure, it may not feel like that on earth where
you can get brain freeze from ice cream before

00:06:36.030 --> 00:06:38.970
burning your mouth on apple pie.

00:06:38.970 --> 00:06:43.980
But on a general level, like if you cut the
universe up into cubic light years, they are

00:06:43.980 --> 00:06:47.110
all roughly the same temperature.

00:06:47.110 --> 00:06:48.110
The reason this is strange is that 14 billion
years may seem like a long time for us but

00:06:48.110 --> 00:06:52.220
on a cosmic scale, the universe is still a
toddler, wetting the bed and drawing all over

00:06:52.220 --> 00:06:54.050
the walls in crayon.

00:06:54.050 --> 00:07:00.000
The big bang was astonishingly hot so you’d
expect there to be more hot pockets, and I’m

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:03.380
not talking about that snack that sort of
resembles food.

00:07:03.380 --> 00:07:08.120
Some theorist have wondered if maybe there
is some unknown energy that caused an expansion

00:07:08.120 --> 00:07:13.330
faster than the speed of light, which would
explain how the temperature evened out so

00:07:13.330 --> 00:07:14.330
quickly.

00:07:14.330 --> 00:07:20.300
Niayesh Afshordi and his team came up with
the idea that perhaps our 3D universe was

00:07:20.300 --> 00:07:28.290
not only in a 4D bulk universe but one that
created 4D stars.

00:07:28.290 --> 00:07:35.190
And if you have 4D stars, these can collapse
into 4D black holes so perhaps we are like

00:07:35.190 --> 00:07:39.940
sheet of paper, wrapped around the event horizon
of one of these black holes, sucking up the

00:07:39.940 --> 00:07:43.060
enormous energy that would be surrounding
us.

00:07:43.060 --> 00:07:48.010
Thanks to the work of great minds all over
the world, we are learning more and more about

00:07:48.010 --> 00:07:52.900
what is out there and how it began; from the
quantum madness of the Large Hadron Collider

00:07:52.900 --> 00:07:57.710
to the gravitational waves of dancing black
holes found by the advanced LIGO project.

00:07:57.710 --> 00:08:03.889
But perhaps the true origins of the universe
are something we will never be able to prove

00:08:03.889 --> 00:08:06.580
and maybe we don’t really want to know.

00:08:06.580 --> 00:08:12.900
I mean, how would you feel if you knew that
we were just a tiny, insignificant planet,

00:08:12.900 --> 00:08:18.169
in one of many tiny insignificant universes?

00:08:18.169 --> 00:08:25.100
Or if the big bang was just the sneeze of
some 8 dimensional cat… actually I’m pretty

00:08:25.100 --> 00:08:48.360
fine with that last one.

